From 0000000000000000000000000000000000000000 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: theprincesajjad <188352564+theprincesajjad@users.noreply.github.com>
Date: Sun, 22 Nov 2026 09:12:37 -0500
Subject: [PATCH] Accept file attachments of any size

Upstream caps a file attachment at 50 MiB in the shared contract, so the
server, the web client and every reader of a thread enforce it.

The upload limit is now the host's disk. A file past the old limit goes
up as resumable chunks on the existing signed upload URL, because one
request cannot carry gigabytes: Node drops a request after five minutes,
and a dropped connection would restart from zero.

Official desktop and mobile builds still reject a file attachment whose
sizeBytes exceeds 50 MiB, as would an official server reading this
database after a rollback. So the stored and transmitted sizeBytes stays
clamped and the true size travels in actualSizeBytes, a key those readers
ignore. In memory the attachment carries its true size, so no caller
changes.

Claiming an upload for a thread now clones it copy-on-write where the
filesystem can, instead of writing a second full copy.

Co-Authored-By: Claude Fable 5.2 <noreply@anthropic.com>
